assistant: Subject: parse epoch-millisecond created_at in get_order

Starting with vendor API version 2026-10-01, `created_at` comes back as a number of milliseconds since 1970 (e.g. `1792142857943`), not the ISO-8601 string the published spec describes. `get_order` turned that number into a string and passed it to `Date.parse`, which returned NaN. The `|| 0` fallback then turned that into `1970-01-01T00:00:00.000Z`. Now, when `created_at` is a number, the handler builds the Date straight from it, so o-5001 returns `2026-10-16T09:27:37.943Z`, matching upstream. String values still go through the old code, so the v1 fixtures give the same output as before. Typecheck, lint and tests all pass. I left the `|| 0` fallback alone to keep the change small, but an unparseable value from a future change would still show up silently as 1970 instead of being flagged. It's worth replacing.

Subject: parse epoch-millisecond created_at in get_order

Starting with vendor API version 2026-10-01, `created_at` comes back as a number of milliseconds since 1970 (e.g. `1792142857943`), not the ISO-8601 string the published spec describes. `get_order` turned that number into a string and passed it to `Date.parse`, which returned NaN. The `|| 0` fallback then turned that into `1970-01-01T00:00:00.000Z`. Now, when `created_at` is a number, the handler builds the Date straight from it, so o-5001 returns `2026-10-16T09:27:37.943Z`, matching upstream. String values still go through the old code, so the v1 fixtures give the same output as before. Typecheck, lint and tests all pass. I left the `|| 0` fallback alone to keep the change small, but an unparseable value from a future change would still show up silently as 1970 instead of being flagged. It's worth replacing.
